  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe 19Z weather map shows 1002mb surface low over WY and northern CO \nand 1034mb high pressure across the Mid-Atlantic region. As we \nmoving into the evening hours we'll see the low to our southwest \nbecoming better organized and deepen to 994mb over northeastern CO \nby 06Z Monday. Light to steady moderate rain will begin to surge \nnortheast across the forecast area late this evening and overnight. \n\nExpect less of a cool down tonight, with temperatures remaining in \nthe 50s. due to plenty of clouds and warmer air pushing in ahead of \nthe surface low. Winds will also remain slightly mixed, with no \nsignificant temperature inversion setting up overnight. Record or \nnear record warm low temperatures are forecast tonight, particularly \nat ABR where the record is a weak 44 degrees from 1990. Temperatures \nwill struggle to drop below 55 degrees tonight over much of the area.\n\nA low level 40-55kt jet will set up over our western zones \novernight, central forecast area Monday morning, and over the east \ninto early Monday afternoon. The low level jet will be under a \nstratus deck. Unlike the overnight, there will be less mixing \nduring the day. \n\nOne concern to the steady all-day precipitation will be a dry slot \nthat is looking more and more likely 12-14Z from around PIR south \nand expanding across much of the area south of U.S. Highway 12. Most \nof the CAMs keep the precipitation pivoting over Corson County, but \nwe will see this trends over time. Some convective attributes start \nto show up Monday afternoon into the evening hours over portions of \ncentral SD. We remain in the general thunderstorm outlook from SPC \nfor tonight, with the line shifting mainly east of the Eagle Butte \nto Eureka area. So, if any thunderstorms do develop in the dry slot \non on the edge, they will have the potential to produce brief heavy \nrain. PW values max out around 1.5 to 1.6in starting over our \nsouthern counties at 06Z Monday and expanding across northeastern SD \nand west central MN by 12Z Monday before exiting northeast late \nMonday afternoon. These PW values are above normal for this time of \nyear, and closer to records for the first half of October.\n\nA tight pressure gradient Monday afternoon-evening and pressure \nrises will peak over western SD will help promote strong gusty \nwinds. A Wind Advisory may be needed either late afternoon, if we \nend up mixing the stronger winds down, or later Monday evening for \nareas near and west of the Missouri River. It still looks like winds \nwill gust 45-55mph Monday night into early Tuesday, lingering into \nTuesday afternoon over Jones/Lyman Counties.\n\nTotal precipitation wise, there is now a larger range in the 25th-\n75th percentiles, showing the at the spread is becoming larger and \ntherefore confidence in any specific number is decreasing. Much of \nthis is due to the differences in the main camps with the GEFS \nsolutions being farther west, GEPS farther east, and EC Ensembles \nbeing somewhere in the middle. The differences in the operational \nGFS/Canadian/EC are large enough to highlight this lower confidence, \nespecially where a dry slot may set up. While the steady rain ends \nTuesday, we still have chances of rain lingering into Wednesday \nevening over portions of northeastern SD/west central MN. Dry \nweather is then forecast through next Sunday.\n\nAfter highs in the 60s today and Monday, highs will top out in the \n40s through the rest of the work week.",
